[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14aab469-1f71-5582-a61c-34ca084d5f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551707} {:answer/id #uuid "216e442c-c072-5728-a705-afb102d6fbea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551739} {:answer/id #uuid "285237b4-7d17-5283-a8ae-a41635f56b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551757} {:answer/id #uuid "295f06f2-f22f-5444-b217-5a8a03ad09ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551759} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d3096c-e5b3-55c4-80ea-469e433551fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3d3096c-e5b3-55c4-80ea-469e433551fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552060} :answer-field/id #uuid "831aaab2-d476-5739-b94e-9e3263993fc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3f0420a1-666b-5191-842f-3d2bdb44e681" :question/math-academy-id "q-141440" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{5}{13}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{7}-b\\sqrt{2}}{39},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{5}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{12}{13})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})-(-\\frac{5}{13})(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12\\sqrt{2}}{39}+\\frac{5\\sqrt{7}}{39} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{7}-12\\sqrt{2}}{39}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=5,b=12,$ and $a+b=17.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e66c7b2-03cf-5b40-acd9-587dadb9ac9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551818} {:answer/id #uuid "6ddca841-e0d9-56b3-8b19-47941defcf69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551925} {:answer/id #uuid "8d53cd2a-8bc8-5203-a4b8-0c7848792908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552009} {:answer/id #uuid "b87397f3-ec91-5023-b6e1-c9d09c5dfccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552110} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd58ea1-aff0-5d22-8f67-8c8bb8e6db07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ddca841-e0d9-56b3-8b19-47941defcf69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551925} :answer-field/id #uuid "afbfba15-89cd-5f8b-8d11-e527ad3d57cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43bdc32f-8f5e-5fac-84f7-776394b1cc9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-119592" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{2}-b\\sqrt{14}}{8},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+(\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{8}+\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-1\\sqrt{14}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=3,b=1,$ and $a+b=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a31093c-3bc1-5604-8b68-60bb29456071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21 \\\\ 25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551690} {:answer/id #uuid "1c236ae5-4aa5-5314-b3d7-d9f14364f42d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}62 \\\\ 45\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551725} {:answer/id #uuid "55bb8ae9-83ab-5bd6-b3fa-7323cb3b3ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}62 & 45\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551870} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b3471b-58e2-538a-96d3-b94d24b16af3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 31\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552101} {:answer/id #uuid "f9195545-d1e3-513f-91e6-f5228e90b375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & 31\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c236ae5-4aa5-5314-b3d7-d9f14364f42d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}62 \\\\ 45\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551725} :answer-field/id #uuid "560cd508-cebe-5335-8021-8a9e70cdc673" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47594296-da7d-5903-b008-c5f5265a8817" :question/math-academy-id "q-269489" :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)={(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)=\\frac{1}{2}A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2}A)}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{1}{2}A)}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & ={A}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b) \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 34\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}62 \\\\ 45\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13e18065-a00c-590c-bd30-b3697aef1703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551704} {:answer/id #uuid "321078fd-ddc7-5fa9-8ffd-02d0df3acd41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551784} {:answer/id #uuid "367fba42-a7e2-593f-bdf2-3db31fe9789a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551796} {:answer/id #uuid "5b80064a-5abe-5355-884e-5d229396f3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551885} {:answer/id #uuid "c88bd8a5-d479-58a6-8d0c-28fb55f5c168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c88bd8a5-d479-58a6-8d0c-28fb55f5c168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552143} :answer-field/id #uuid "77b4248f-fe82-5a6e-b664-fd773a876604" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50ab1b1c-89b6-5bde-a50d-34d9fce11246" :question/math-academy-id "q-141443" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{3}+b\\sqrt{5}}{20},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(2\\sqrt{5})^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{20}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{20}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5})-(\\frac{1}{4})(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{20}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}+2\\sqrt{5}}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=5,b=2,$ and $a+b=7.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e87a69a-83da-50aa-90f7-3e2c57996375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{87}{{6}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551730} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8a985f-6cf4-5d99-a382-0c90a98f52a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{6}{{78}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551848} {:answer/id #uuid "6db750dc-fe88-55f5-8fc6-6406faf86ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{78}{{13}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551924} {:answer/id #uuid "9bee3c06-979d-5760-b511-9139eafe8ac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{6}{{13}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552043} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0cfecd-0318-5e34-9d02-cc04ef5ef74e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{78}{{6}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca0cfecd-0318-5e34-9d02-cc04ef5ef74e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{78}{{6}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552148} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed7fe9ae-7173-5401-b140-6234f32b66d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e4e0f75-9894-567b-a1a8-c012ad934066" :question/math-academy-id "q-73268" :question/problem "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence given by\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=\\frac{f(n)}{6},\\,\\,f(1)=13,\\,\\,n\\ge 1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+1)=\\frac{f(n)}{6}=\\frac{1}{6}f(n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)\\cdot {r}^{n-1},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $f(1)$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $f(1)=13.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=r\\cdot f(n)\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r=\\frac{1}{6}.$\n\nSubstituting $f(1)=13$ and $r=\\frac{1}{6}$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =13\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =13\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =13\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{-1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n} \\\\  & =13\\cdot \\frac{6}{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n} \\\\  & =78\\cdot \\frac{1}{{6}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{78}{{6}^{n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n)=\\frac{78}{{6}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fd779df-49e5-5e25-be2d-c22bba641aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551969} {:answer/id #uuid "8b795d79-1d06-5f11-b31e-dc17f7053fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552006} {:answer/id #uuid "936f28df-a16a-538a-be6a-eec59f948467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552022} {:answer/id #uuid "e76101c8-badb-5987-9480-28be10e44a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552205} {:answer/id #uuid "f696b631-7dc2-5a31-af20-dff99b948f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b795d79-1d06-5f11-b31e-dc17f7053fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552006} :answer-field/id #uuid "de040dcd-65e4-5530-8e4b-808b33262dec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "77e70b3f-8704-511e-87c9-d67cfcbe9ec9" :question/math-academy-id "q-141441" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{5}{7},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=-\\frac{a\\sqrt{2}+b}{14},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{7})(-\\frac{1}{2})-(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{14}-\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{14} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}+5}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=6,b=5,$ and $a+b=11.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7bf6b1-d530-59ea-9dc8-7b99db3d0094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4y}{3}, & \\,2<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551692} {:answer/id #uuid "165a73fa-f469-5a44-86ef-009948eb5611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}, & \\,1<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551713} {:answer/id #uuid "1fc1534a-6a01-51a4-9c6e-4184dd7e0bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{6}, & \\,1<y<2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551733} {:answer/id #uuid "b5dc456d-f743-5286-851a-c035feeffac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{6}, & \\,2<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552105} {:answer/id #uuid "d167a7a8-34d5-59c3-b949-f001717e3bd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3}, & \\,2<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5dc456d-f743-5286-851a-c035feeffac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{6}, & \\,2<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552105} :answer-field/id #uuid "69f2e9ff-fa41-50ab-a63c-2d1d1f490067" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8460a557-83b6-5efd-a8a5-c94e63881b6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-151985" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{3},\\, & 1<x<2 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=2X,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ in terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=2X$ is a strictly increasing function of $X.$  Moreover, for $1<x<2,$ we have $2<y<4.$\n\n![](images/60/60342277a025cb26cebab2ac534fad4aa1b49fc2285571c303c4e829eb464606.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $2<y<4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2X\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le \\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $2<y<4,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2(\\frac{y}{2})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{6}, & \\,2<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552476 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30da5234-4042-5fab-bc17-28a88bfb9de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-26 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551781} {:answer/id #uuid "37e4fbe8-c0f7-58ed-9b71-06218e634007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}28 & -16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551799} {:answer/id #uuid "868cbcd3-651b-5e03-a865-b0dfaf92d893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 32\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551989} {:answer/id #uuid "d0501560-e02a-5299-a647-3098cd11d592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}52 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552158} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c321cc-baeb-5602-885c-ed6669f5d604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0501560-e02a-5299-a647-3098cd11d592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}52 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552158} :answer-field/id #uuid "6730a75f-f272-5493-a8bf-085f7b4def46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9669b6bb-a27d-584f-935a-b35adacb11c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-269476" :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}52 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01664bcb-25bc-5604-ac5b-7905582c2b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4-6\\sqrt{2}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551667} {:answer/id #uuid "2b869f32-d6ca-57fc-b45e-d252c7e98fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3+8\\sqrt{2}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551766} {:answer/id #uuid "41b89c7d-25df-5525-b5cb-ba7faba112c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\sqrt{2}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551822} {:answer/id #uuid "53b3b239-af76-5af9-8ec7-54d956c4236a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3-8\\sqrt{2}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551862} {:answer/id #uuid "d5703300-df93-5e24-bec4-819d88120806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4+6\\sqrt{2}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b869f32-d6ca-57fc-b45e-d252c7e98fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3+8\\sqrt{2}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551766} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3e78b0b-13cf-51a9-87c2-6116b81037d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97e789b8-dcb1-527a-b969-3a0ccc1e1111" :question/math-academy-id "q-143842" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{2}^{2}(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{15}+\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+8\\sqrt{2}}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4060772b-3de8-5534-b56c-8b7a37476bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{7}{{3}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551821} {:answer/id #uuid "53b52c4e-fecf-5906-b0d1-f38305a89134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=3\\cdot {7}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551863} {:answer/id #uuid "63d5d4e3-2de8-53a3-8ffc-a054da079d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {3}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551901} {:answer/id #uuid "b2bb07b2-92a7-55ae-ba89-d2cfd3c82595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{3}{{7}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552099} {:answer/id #uuid "c94b2131-8119-57cb-89b4-783c4f703e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{21}{{7}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c94b2131-8119-57cb-89b4-783c4f703e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{21}{{7}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552145} :answer-field/id #uuid "723b6e58-02ae-5c02-8cb3-0f391e390f10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98925317-9d30-5e69-bd45-e05c5755cffe" :question/math-academy-id "q-44987" :question/problem "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{7},\\,\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,\\,n\\ge 1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is ${a}_{1}=3.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r=\\frac{1}{7}.$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=3$ and $r=\\frac{1}{7}$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{-1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 7\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{n}}{{7}^{n}}) \\\\  & =21\\cdot \\frac{1}{{7}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{{7}^{n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{21}{{7}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e7e8d4-1f2c-5731-9bb1-e3ca505e33e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-\\frac{16}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551671} {:answer/id #uuid "3396d8a5-3041-5b5a-9e47-d23a76bcc657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{16}{5}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{4})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551788} {:answer/id #uuid "4c334bbd-0f7b-5f52-a4a2-f06688b4334a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{4})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551846} {:answer/id #uuid "7582d12c-3842-52cf-9c33-8825aa815a9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551942} {:answer/id #uuid "84778c37-5699-5591-895f-445665b6e0ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{16}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03e7e8d4-1f2c-5731-9bb1-e3ca505e33e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-\\frac{16}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551671} :answer-field/id #uuid "36e03764-6444-5a97-a79c-a332453344e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a2f8eb3-e0d7-5d85-8fb5-62ab15cba80d" :question/math-academy-id "q-101752" :question/problem "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{4},\\,\\,{a}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\,n\\ge 1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is ${a}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{5}.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{5}$ and $r=\\frac{1}{4}$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{-1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{4}{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\cdot 4}{5\\cdot 1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{16}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552481 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee7a38c5-9093-561e-b5af-54b8e86b60c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{17\\sqrt{2}}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee7a38c5-9093-561e-b5af-54b8e86b60c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{17\\sqrt{2}}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552222} :answer-field/id #uuid "b69a755b-9199-59fe-b15b-6dc54413bd48" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e4e39a8-9ee3-514c-8a99-c8c4e6788313" :question/math-academy-id "q-325885" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{5}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{5}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{12}{13})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(\\frac{5}{13})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12\\sqrt{2}}{26}-\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{26} \\\\  & =-\\frac{17\\sqrt{2}}{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c48d543-2d3c-51bb-a7bf-0270b3661c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{2}{{6}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551847} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8329a8-9240-5aad-b604-d9ff98436aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)={24}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552041} {:answer/id #uuid "cc85700b-91d3-5f37-a0fd-d0b78d1f06ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{2}{{24}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552154} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c2c41c-0f75-5fa4-a688-22bb4ee3f293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{4}{{6}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552199} {:answer/id #uuid "e877ea80-a0f4-50cc-b19e-fc100855d57b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{24}{{6}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e877ea80-a0f4-50cc-b19e-fc100855d57b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{24}{{6}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552207} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d6b8a21-70e2-5b57-a0a2-6cb7c4f3a1b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a040fd0d-33b9-5cd2-ba84-4a64f3c5606d" :question/math-academy-id "q-109880" :question/problem "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence given by\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=\\frac{f(n)}{6},\\,\\,f(1)=4,\\,\\,n\\ge 1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+1)=\\frac{f(n)}{6}=\\frac{1}{6}f(n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)\\cdot {r}^{n-1},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $f(1)$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $f(1)=4.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=r\\cdot f(n)\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r=\\frac{1}{6}.$\n\nSubstituting $f(1)=4$ and $r=\\frac{1}{6}$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =4\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{-1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{6}{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n} \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\frac{1}{{6}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{{6}^{n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n)=\\frac{24}{{6}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d42fa31-693c-5e63-8b30-f714f163cf61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551774} {:answer/id #uuid "5a97f892-a6ec-5102-a2f8-b11ba307ea5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551880} {:answer/id #uuid "788d4fd5-7983-5bde-ac02-62cb31dfdb02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551948} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd545ee-d36f-5cc5-9368-52d6c0d580da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551960} {:answer/id #uuid "ceda2cc6-35df-59c0-b345-80992bffb35d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bd545ee-d36f-5cc5-9368-52d6c0d580da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551960} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1fc0cd0-57f3-5df9-9d82-31c607584763" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c696854b-d1cc-50f5-8398-3fba96da4739" :question/math-academy-id "q-127063" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=xi+\\frac{1}{4}\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }32{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }32(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1}{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+16t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(0+32\\pi -1)-(0-1) \\\\  & =32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a3bc49-b244-599b-b0ff-1cbd8f81ac70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n+1)=-\\frac{1}{2}f(n),\\,f(1)=\\frac{3}{4},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551687} {:answer/id #uuid "325d1297-2972-550e-b88d-db16dc9819fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n+1)=\\frac{1}{2}f(n),\\,f(1)=\\frac{3}{2},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551785} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc4548f-c1f5-5340-802b-4754d9b73c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n+1)=\\frac{1}{2}f(n),\\,f(1)=-\\frac{3}{2},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551812} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e204ed-7f08-5596-a06e-74bc1aaea2ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n+1)=-\\frac{1}{2}f(n),\\,f(1)=\\frac{3}{2},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552192} {:answer/id #uuid "ec092f6f-dfdb-527f-a5bf-640a0c4dff59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n+1)=\\frac{3}{4}f(n),\\,f(1)=-\\frac{2}{3},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3e204ed-7f08-5596-a06e-74bc1aaea2ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n+1)=-\\frac{1}{2}f(n),\\,f(1)=\\frac{3}{2},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552192} :answer-field/id #uuid "eff995c5-b742-5fa7-b249-c06283c96fda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfd63bcb-672d-5a91-b2cf-d24e8a457ef0" :question/math-academy-id "q-109868" :question/problem "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is $f(n)=\\frac{6}{(-2)^{n+1}}$ for $n\\ge 1.$  What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=r\\cdot f(n).\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term $f(1).$ The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =\\frac{6}{(-2)^{1+1}}=\\frac{6}{4}=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{6}{(-2)^{2+1}}=\\frac{6}{(-8)}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)}=\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{4})}{(\\frac{3}{2})}=-\\frac{6}{12}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+1) & =-\\frac{1}{2}f(n),\\,f(1)=\\frac{3}{2},\\,n\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53f11dd5-3815-523d-8b4f-218a0c887321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551865} {:answer/id #uuid "76d37b1f-0cae-52e4-bbc9-ab6fd6b7061a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y},\\, & y<0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551946} {:answer/id #uuid "7a5a5e16-2b43-5e40-8cb3-9fe6c9bc2a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & -1<y<0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551954} {:answer/id #uuid "e933345b-51e3-5f41-b177-21de967b42a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y},\\, & y>0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552210} {:answer/id #uuid "ee37ca3b-30b3-5807-a624-f62ac29989e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53f11dd5-3815-523d-8b4f-218a0c887321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551865} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea541e01-5fa9-5c1a-9bd5-70920b6a9e22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f3a2a8f5-8f7b-5578-9df3-f4e28dce2d10" :question/math-academy-id "q-110130" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\, & x>1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\frac{1}{X},$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that the possible values of $X$ are $x\\in (1,\\infty ),$ and $Y=\\frac{1}{X}$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X$ when $X>1.$\n Moreover, for $x>1,$ we have $0<y<1.$\n\n![](images/16/16f3dc78752c8cfcb6b6968e3d57bb3639a4205e2a32dfd569c8c2ca3c29a589.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(\\frac{1}{X}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\ge \\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =1-{F}_{X}(\\frac{1}{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<1,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0-(-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{1}{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{(\\frac{1}{y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552782 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "281698df-9249-5da9-9368-9b2f93cd8848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}2 t \\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552791} {:answer/id #uuid "455025c2-db51-556e-a3e6-9fa4b4a79dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}t \\left(4 - 6 t^{3}\\right) \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552799} {:answer/id #uuid "4c1ecf92-1397-5d44-97e4-f143aa9d8c69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}2 t \\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552801} {:answer/id #uuid "4f811054-64ac-515c-b215-4b187de7bc5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}2 \\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552805} {:answer/id #uuid "a60d9d4a-69db-5a5f-9245-b3924cba5128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}2 t \\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a60d9d4a-69db-5a5f-9245-b3924cba5128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}2 t \\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552830} :answer-field/id #uuid "0763297f-2785-5418-b00d-ee5ade6a337c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "10654151-77bf-5b54-8ad8-7a9ddd7ea1a1" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 2 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 - 3 y^{3}$ traversed between the points $(2,0)$ and $(-79,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 - 3 t^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 2 - 3 t^{3},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 - 3 t^{3})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(- 9 t^{2})i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(- 9 t^{2})^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} y,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2\\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2}t^{1} \\\\ &=2 t \\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}(2 t \\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}2 t \\left(2 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552800 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2275c2b9-bd7c-5d72-90c8-7b26275f195e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 t^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552788} {:answer/id #uuid "36e310df-bff1-5173-9e88-7181749cd810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 t^{5}}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552795} {:answer/id #uuid "77b420d0-67c2-528a-91a7-5fcbbe514595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552812} {:answer/id #uuid "7aee2490-8ebd-5fff-aeba-dd54ccd94877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 t^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552814} {:answer/id #uuid "ac98de36-83ed-5aef-b75d-338dc0b2dbdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "768 t^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aee2490-8ebd-5fff-aeba-dd54ccd94877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 t^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552814} :answer-field/id #uuid "e31bb3e8-a091-5beb-bbef-ce8a5745dea3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4996d341-4274-5583-88ff-0f580b30aea8" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C 48 x^{2} y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\int_{1}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $4x=y$ traversed between the points $(\\frac{1}{4},1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$. Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n4x=y\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,x=\\frac{y}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{t}{4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle\\frac{t}{4},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\frac{t}{4})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(\\frac{1}{4})i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(\\frac{1}{4})^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}+1} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{16}} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=48 x^{2} y^{3},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=48\\left(\\frac{t}{4}\\right)^2t^{3} \\\\ &=48\\left(\\frac{t^2}{16}\\right)t^{3} \\\\ &=3 t^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 48 x^{2} y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{2}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}(3 t^{5})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\int_{1}^{2}3 t^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552818 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23197b07-5a7b-53de-9d9e-c615db2ae1e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}t^{2} \\left(2 t^{3} - 8\\right) \\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552789} {:answer/id #uuid "2df87d88-57a2-5c89-9a85-320711d02d09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552793} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0929a7-5013-5e55-aa1e-ae864b350074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552815} {:answer/id #uuid "d080d482-7b0f-5090-b506-abea27a84bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}2 \\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552839} {:answer/id #uuid "ed952ab0-5dec-52bf-9c72-9b41ef3af6ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed952ab0-5dec-52bf-9c72-9b41ef3af6ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552847} :answer-field/id #uuid "c85e1c25-dace-5d43-9b45-c16fb0df76d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7ed925b2-2bfc-5cd1-979d-0167eb4f42c0" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 2 x^{2} y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=y^{3} - 4$ traversed between the points $(-4,0)$ and $(23,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=t^{3} - 4.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t^{3} - 4,\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{3} - 4)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(3 t^{2})i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(3 t^{2})^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} y^{2},$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2\\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2}t^{2} \\\\ &=2 t^{2} \\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 x^{2} y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}(2 t^{2} \\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(t^{3} - 4\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552819 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ce1b2b9-3b87-52e0-a1f6-172a88bf7b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 t^{3} \\left(2 - 2 t\\right)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552802} {:answer/id #uuid "680810eb-e33e-5d7a-ac2d-3e3d2bcc7f23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552809} {:answer/id #uuid "b7a2acf9-a41f-5160-8dc1-756db6ef76d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 t^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552834} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c023e8-5a47-5179-b7eb-afe7ce12e7ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 t^{3} \\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552842} {:answer/id #uuid "f3bd6f10-f9f4-538e-913c-d22618c6f106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 t^{2} \\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0c023e8-5a47-5179-b7eb-afe7ce12e7ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 t^{3} \\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552842} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ecb885d-e56e-5e60-9407-9e9e3fa26bfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86be1e77-b581-5d25-9650-3cc553d658e6" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C 4 x^{2} y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\sqrt{5}\\int_{2}^{4}\\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $x=2 y + 2$ traversed between the points $(6,2)$ and $(10,4)$. Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 t + 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 2 t + 2,\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 2\\le t\\le 4\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 4$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t + 2)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(2)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(2)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} y^{3},$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=4\\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{2}t^{3} \\\\ &=4 t^{3} \\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 4 x^{2} y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{2}^{4}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{2}^{4}(4 t^{3} \\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\sqrt{5}\\int_{2}^{4}4 t^{3} \\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552826 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0260f071-3897-545d-bb02-440d61d15330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 - 5 t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552779} {:answer/id #uuid "17052b32-b23e-5b70-b603-b912587c5543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 - t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552785} {:answer/id #uuid "19c6da5f-d3b5-56f5-94b4-f6ad26195c2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7 t - 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552787} {:answer/id #uuid "87c31bd7-b6ca-5532-b4da-aad42b84c763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 - 3 t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552820} {:answer/id #uuid "d7fb372a-4d60-5b14-bc9e-3501041766f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 5 t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0260f071-3897-545d-bb02-440d61d15330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 - 5 t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552779} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ee0746c-4281-5a79-87b4-bd2fbd506c40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99c0214e-a2d8-5339-8aee-c36838439a0d" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C x - 2 y\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\sqrt{10}\\int_{0}^{4}\\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $\\frac{x}{4}=1 - \\frac{3 y}{4}$ traversed between the points $(4,0)$ and $(-8,4)$. Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=1 - \\frac{3 y}{4}\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,x=4 - 3 y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=4 - 3 t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 4 - 3 t,\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 0\\le t\\le 4\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 4$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(4 - 3 t)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(-3)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(-3)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{9+1} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=x - 2 y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=1\\left(4 - 3 t\\right)-2t \\\\ &=4 - 5 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x - 2 y\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{4}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{4}(4 - 5 t)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\int_{0}^{4}(4 - 5 t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "417a2e2c-c99e-5b7e-89a5-04fd8801fb79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552798} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3d15a8-46f5-5bf1-811c-a4f19b6a7124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}t^{2} \\left(4 t^{2} + 6\\right) \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552813} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc7687e-5dff-5e5e-97a9-d31743837c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}2 \\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552816} {:answer/id #uuid "c023fc4c-620c-5620-a1bb-9b3840ec1fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552836} {:answer/id #uuid "ed840fbf-dc53-5f4f-83cf-efc910b3cde0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "417a2e2c-c99e-5b7e-89a5-04fd8801fb79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552798} :answer-field/id #uuid "1224e6c3-08af-592f-b40f-08f63238075d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9eafb873-c81e-5383-b266-5700676268cc" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 2 x^{2} y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y^{2} + 3$ traversed between the points $(5,1)$ and $(21,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 t^{2} + 3.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 2 t^{2} + 3,\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t^{2} + 3)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(4 t)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(4 t)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} y^{2},$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2\\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2}t^{2} \\\\ &=2 t^{2} \\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 x^{2} y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{3}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}(2 t^{2} \\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}2 t^{2} \\left(2 t^{2} + 3\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05820e9b-5d3f-5f00-8ab1-5cb728ad058c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}t \\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552780} {:answer/id #uuid "3912c436-323c-5514-82b7-0a551fe02d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}t^{5} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552796} {:answer/id #uuid "5534bb00-f895-5c7c-97e6-684d212578d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{6}t^{4} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552806} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a0976d-6042-5f97-9145-cb3e806a614e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}\\sqrt{10} t^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552828} {:answer/id #uuid "c7400a6a-8f3d-57bb-bce2-f39b5a9cf40c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}t^{4} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7400a6a-8f3d-57bb-bce2-f39b5a9cf40c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}t^{4} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552837} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ae4cf2f-2882-5737-907d-832d0c62119c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa8602a5-ab99-5ec1-a115-7e29b5ef7be8" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C y^{2} e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=3 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(6,e^{2})$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le e^{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le e^{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(\\frac{3}{t})i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(\\frac{3}{t})^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{t^2}+1} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{t^{2} + 9}{t^2}} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}}{|t|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=y^{2} e^{x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=1\\left(e^{3\\ln t}\\right)t^{2} \\\\ &=1\\left(t^{3}\\right)t^{2} \\\\ &=t^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C y^{2} e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}(t^{5})\\cdot \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}t^{4} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552833 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e8e6cf-6f2d-52c7-9466-9bfe3b894881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{4}4 \\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{36 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552786} {:answer/id #uuid "65b7db08-9576-514c-8058-8a41c58bbf69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{4}4 t \\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552807} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8131b6-7baf-5780-9fa9-322bc7065bf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{4}t \\left(8 t^{3} + 8\\right) \\sqrt{36 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552821} {:answer/id #uuid "8fba40cf-cdeb-5e12-8408-18012db7f78f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{4}4 t \\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{36 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552824} {:answer/id #uuid "9370dc17-2d30-55db-b451-380c43d516d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{4}4 t \\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{4 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fba40cf-cdeb-5e12-8408-18012db7f78f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{4}4 t \\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{36 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552824} :answer-field/id #uuid "26a99906-0888-518b-a404-298ec7ba3467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "add2d24a-5255-5b7c-82c3-e44bf8b960be" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 4 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y^{3} + 2$ traversed between the points $(4,1)$ and $(130,4)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 t^{3} + 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 2 t^{3} + 2,\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le 4\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 4$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t^{3} + 2)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(6 t^{2})i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(6 t^{2})^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{36 t^{4} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} y,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=4\\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2}t^{1} \\\\ &=4 t \\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 4 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{4}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{4}(4 t \\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{36 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{4}4 t \\left(2 t^{3} + 2\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{36 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124cbff1-5e98-5d16-ab1f-cc5dd9bcb262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}4 t^{2} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552784} {:answer/id #uuid "73be819e-c547-5ed8-8ad1-7fbb722f68d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{4}4 t^{2} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552811} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8d803f-dbce-540f-ae8b-e4bb8ad0e1e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}4 \\sqrt{t^{2} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552822} {:answer/id #uuid "b95ec9fe-f64a-57bc-b94e-843559920235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}4 t^{3} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552835} {:answer/id #uuid "e4084919-f343-5591-a6f6-3cbf2ccd120c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}4 \\sqrt{5} t^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "124cbff1-5e98-5d16-ab1f-cc5dd9bcb262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}4 t^{2} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552784} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ec0c910-12f6-5049-8da0-f391acb979fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da6a50f8-dbf0-5c57-9f91-2fc2ad40dfd3" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 4 y e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\ln y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(4,e^{2})$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=\\ln y\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,x=2 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le e^{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le e^{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(\\frac{2}{t})i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(\\frac{2}{t})^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{t^2}+1} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4 y e^{x}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=4 y e^{x} \\\\ &=4\\cdot t^{1}e^{2\\ln t} \\\\ &=4\\cdot t^{1}\\left(t^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=4 t^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 4 y e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}(4 t^{3})\\cdot \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{4}{t^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}4 t^{2} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e8e17dd-b1fd-5b63-9132-ad3a7dd6d991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}5 t \\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552797} {:answer/id #uuid "4f69e9c5-3b56-564c-bca3-98cc70036ce6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}t \\left(15 - 15 t^{3}\\right) \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552804} {:answer/id #uuid "66347383-a7b3-50bf-9101-6c7215d0630b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}5 t \\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552808} {:answer/id #uuid "8f161683-83c0-50bf-a17a-f24ed9e6c9f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}5 t \\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552823} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ca4202-33f0-5ef5-aa09-f8e692b0f39e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}5 \\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f161683-83c0-50bf-a17a-f24ed9e6c9f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}5 t \\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552823} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eb5ae6c-eb61-510c-8183-96be18ef2c0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e43f4bf4-b216-5205-b149-df3643df4708" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 5 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=3 - 3 y^{3}$ traversed between the points $(3,0)$ and $(-78,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=3 - 3 t^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 3 - 3 t^{3},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 - 3 t^{3})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(- 9 t^{2})i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(- 9 t^{2})^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=5 x^{2} y,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=5\\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2}t^{1} \\\\ &=5 t \\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 5 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}(5 t \\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}5 t \\left(3 - 3 t^{3}\\right)^{2} \\sqrt{81 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d53a8c66-6804-52ec-8840-edd3d4929673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d53a8c66-6804-52ec-8840-edd3d4929673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553296} :answer-field/id #uuid "95531b96-24f8-5a49-ac02-3bfee0bf2b70" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "248312e6-be46-5549-b1e3-5f70f146c665" :question/math-academy-id "q-346363" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /8}3{csc}^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=4x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /12$ | $\\pi /8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /3$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /8}3{csc}^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}3{csc}^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u))\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(0+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e0a5b34-4019-5f4a-8375-4303ad8f7b4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552955} {:answer/id #uuid "471c5dea-7288-5a56-958f-067580d15baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=2\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553014} {:answer/id #uuid "a93496e4-97c1-5afd-84fd-e7f7ad97ced5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=4\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553207} {:answer/id #uuid "d4225406-8293-59b3-af72-40ed3480b4c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=4\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553294} {:answer/id #uuid "faba3d5c-7a0d-5929-a968-a619158547ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=6\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4225406-8293-59b3-af72-40ed3480b4c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=4\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553294} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eef6173-ab6c-5870-82d9-2a456dd3ae17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32c3dd6f-214e-5de8-b074-37738b30996f" :question/math-academy-id "q-32475" :question/problem "Calculate the height $h$ of a cylinder given that its volume is $16\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ and the ratio of its height to its radius is $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that  the ratio of the height $h$ to the radius of the base $r$ is $2,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{h}{r} & =2 \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume of a cylinder with radius $r$ and height $h$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $V=16\\pi {\\text{ cm}}^{3}$ and $r=\\frac{h}{2}$ into the formula, we find the value of $h:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\ 16\\pi  & =\\pi {(\\frac{h}{2})}^{2}\\cdot h \\\\ 16 & =\\frac{{h}^{3}}{4} \\\\ {h}^{3} & =64 \\\\ h & =4\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f27cc60-4ad8-593e-9ef5-5d362258831b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{{n}^{4}}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,(j-2)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552888} {:answer/id #uuid "1b99b866-cfcd-5ab3-97e9-6c269fcfe9fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{{n}^{2}}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,{j}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552915} {:answer/id #uuid "265541b8-e6de-5a62-ba09-d145d5094973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{{n}^{2}}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,{j}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552935} {:answer/id #uuid "93fd0fdd-bb74-5b4c-9cce-c52e5835da23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{{n}^{4}}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,{j}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553167} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa60640-75f4-50e0-8054-4fd539a3b520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{{n}^{3}}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,{j}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93fd0fdd-bb74-5b4c-9cce-c52e5835da23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{{n}^{4}}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,{j}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553167} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c5920c5-4b86-5a6d-b9f0-0d98e11b6c2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "37c41f7c-c697-50e0-8b4c-374dcc3f004e" :question/math-academy-id "q-73165" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=(x+2)^{3}$ between $x=-2$ and $x=1$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?" :question/worked-solution "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of subintervals,   ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$, and  $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nThe endpoints indicate that $a=-2$ and $b=1,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-(-2)}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(-2+j\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(-2+\\frac{3j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}{(-2+\\frac{3j}{n}+2)}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{3}{n}{(\\frac{3j}{n})}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{3}{n}\\cdot \\frac{27{j}^{3}}{{n}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{{n}^{4}}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,{j}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553521 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9af70380-e503-50b8-8456-a4d26ba605ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-x(x+1)}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553182} {:answer/id #uuid "a95d1f7e-3ad0-5e4e-bba3-859fd658d6d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{2x+1}(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-x(x+1)}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553208} {:answer/id #uuid "aa65b81f-556d-5759-8d4a-d96a8f580eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{-x(x+1)}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553213} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6f8b25-d391-5520-b35a-d57fb931c15e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{2x+1}(1+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{-x(x+1)}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553216} {:answer/id #uuid "c5520221-3ee7-520d-9922-76e4e5d7251d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{2x+1}(1+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{-x(x+1)}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad6f8b25-d391-5520-b35a-d57fb931c15e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{2x+1}(1+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{-x(x+1)}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553216} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6f2ed98-5841-5a83-8d7d-7fd91c809d2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5339fa9a-9297-5700-9672-1deb7e152857" :question/math-academy-id "q-95527" :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(2x+1),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1)-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(2x+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+1))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(2x+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2x+1}-(-\\frac{1}{(2x+1)\\sqrt{1-(2x+1)^{2}}})\\cdot (2x+1)^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}-4x-1}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2x+1}+\\frac{2}{(2x+1)\\sqrt{-4{x}^{2}-4x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2x+1}(1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-4x(x+1)}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2x+1}(1+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{-x(x+1)}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553524 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16806124-955c-5a66-b53a-1bee733918ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}x+\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552907} {:answer/id #uuid "99b39c3d-5a4b-5d5c-a484-9f196d28568e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{3}{16}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553181} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4a8dfb-5ac3-5a88-bd8f-2e4bb8ba342e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{1}{4}x+\\frac{5}{32}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553218} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c2f18f-3f70-59e1-90ec-f0215820a629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{16}{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553260} {:answer/id #uuid "f7312685-0f5e-5d45-bc1e-7c19be890f56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{1}{4}x+\\frac{3}{16}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae4a8dfb-5ac3-5a88-bd8f-2e4bb8ba342e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{1}{4}x+\\frac{5}{32}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553218} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dc64f2c-cfc9-5565-be92-0803a7ce5d1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "59ed9909-43a7-5d70-8d5b-9f3220bf2775" :question/math-academy-id "q-12438" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{1-x}}$ about $x=0$ is" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{1-x}}=(1-x)^{-1/4} & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-x)^{-5/4} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{5}{16}(1-x)^{-9/4} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =\\frac{5}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =1+(\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot x+(\\frac{5}{16})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4}x+\\frac{5}{32}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63425058-6c08-5e95-8aaa-fe0ae9451531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63425058-6c08-5e95-8aaa-fe0ae9451531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553080} :answer-field/id #uuid "8af448d4-fd7c-5f68-b3a3-6e5b346e24e2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "606f9c6c-5c1e-593b-8109-6427fb98d820" :question/math-academy-id "q-341553" :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x,$ calculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x\\,-\\frac{1}{|x|}\\cdot $ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a product $u(x)\\cdot v(x)$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, we apply the product rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot {(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x\\,+\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x-\\frac{1}{|x|}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553533 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f15eefe-3a94-52eb-b0db-fbe7b7e8c5e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3j+3}{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552926} {:answer/id #uuid "494d84f7-e321-5bbf-9260-a5d994e6bad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{3}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{j}{n}+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553021} {:answer/id #uuid "637e03eb-1b6f-575b-8c6c-dcc21ef08a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n}\\,\\frac{3}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3j}{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553081} {:answer/id #uuid "903605f0-bbb6-51e7-b29a-abff3e1d83c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{3}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3j}{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553155} {:answer/id #uuid "9b0d2c49-0687-55c5-88d3-59452dabce7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\,\\frac{3}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{j}{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "903605f0-bbb6-51e7-b29a-abff3e1d83c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{3}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3j}{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553155} :answer-field/id #uuid "0287ad59-5a09-5d14-b668-9a96a7a6d0a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6c6f0b11-13a1-5912-b416-72a57173178b" :question/math-academy-id "q-116625" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+3)$ between $x=-3$ and $x=0$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?" :question/worked-solution "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of subintervals, ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$, and  $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nThe endpoints indicate that $a=-3$ and $b=0,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{0-(-3)}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(-3+j\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(-3+\\frac{3j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{3}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3+\\frac{3j}{n}+3) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{3}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3j}{n}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1abb5c2a-f907-5ec8-8eaa-045849906fd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{33}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552913} {:answer/id #uuid "23f1850b-9cf1-5ba9-958d-6192a230eab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552932} {:answer/id #uuid "68099384-0fa4-5c9d-b726-604bfb900ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{44}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553087} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd4d1ee-f3c3-561f-b1b0-76bd0e228df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553097} {:answer/id #uuid "8a90a5e9-09f1-5cfd-a38d-7c4b2dd144a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68099384-0fa4-5c9d-b726-604bfb900ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{44}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553087} :answer-field/id #uuid "202889fe-fbdf-5866-9db5-d7e0eb76934f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97290040-c817-56ab-b0de-389c63e1806d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99763" :question/problem "$\\frac{11}{(\\frac{3}{4})}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{(\\frac{3}{4})}=11÷\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $11=\\frac{11}{1}$ and dividing by $\\frac{3}{4}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\frac{4}{3}.$ We can use these facts to simplify the fraction above: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11÷\\frac{3}{4} &= \\frac{11}{1}\\times \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ &= \\frac{11\\times 4}{1\\times 3} \\\\ &= \\frac{44}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{(\\frac{3}{4})}=\\frac{44}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0578ff56-9fb7-5619-a9b1-ed8f2ede6282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{1-x^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0578ff56-9fb7-5619-a9b1-ed8f2ede6282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{1-x^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552874} :answer-field/id #uuid "586bfc89-c55e-5fa0-a3a8-3b72520ea9db" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b39ee0b2-d875-5c07-89bc-5a93b5408c42" :question/math-academy-id "q-341558" :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $f(x)=(x+2)\\,\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x,$ calculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x\\,+$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a product $u(x)\\cdot v(x)$ with $u(x)=x+2$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, we apply the product rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & ={(x+2)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x+(x+2)\\cdot {(\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x\\,+\\,(x+2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x+\\frac{x+2}{1-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553583 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34a0c68b-e9f8-549e-842e-b401217d6da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{i=0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{i\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552968} {:answer/id #uuid "761a9645-004c-5d1a-a018-4e98c7126007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{i\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553106} {:answer/id #uuid "ca16e453-591e-50ca-b9b0-9c6c804546b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{i\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553272} {:answer/id #uuid "dec5872d-a8a0-52d9-b1df-f39709699d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{i=0}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{i\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553311} {:answer/id #uuid "f70e4a83-804d-5d8d-a2bf-8321912f101c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{i\\pi }{4})]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "761a9645-004c-5d1a-a018-4e98c7126007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{i\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553106} :answer-field/id #uuid "80c578ea-f1c8-5e3b-b6d6-b57c333da3a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4c3e43e-fe55-559b-8cc6-a202da2850e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-49330" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})$ between $x=0$ and $x=2$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $4$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n=4$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=2.$ So, the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{4}=\\frac{2}{4}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(a+iΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}f(\\frac{i}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{i\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a6c53df-03c3-5272-b79f-e92b2df68cc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6b/6b4dec42ca9c8a54be8aa13f611907e5c7e3e4bce5e177c2cc5970bc2cf69735.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553974} {:answer/id #uuid "41d3f9e0-0839-5481-977d-ea32ab572300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/88/88b831093808dba6820a2f8faeb3770ecf50f66ef56a0ef34887622eee639a30.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553989} {:answer/id #uuid "6f1cbf10-0914-558c-a35e-1b176c12da6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/83/839406ead3c7deda0ec0f783c01e1c99aca8877a7ba0ecfdb03f7adb883706fb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554089} {:answer/id #uuid "8901eefd-bfc8-5877-be0f-8a92a3ab82dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/17/175b02e0003de363b989f13f7ad4082ea6d87b56f8cc616605328ba775db5be2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554156} {:answer/id #uuid "db644941-4b47-5d57-9948-d42917f87daa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/58/587a9e679f9d446c8ae57ec93fadcb69ed423fd4803abd2150cd845ac1dc9558.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8901eefd-bfc8-5877-be0f-8a92a3ab82dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/17/175b02e0003de363b989f13f7ad4082ea6d87b56f8cc616605328ba775db5be2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554156} :answer-field/id #uuid "94669996-364b-5fcd-ac9d-e6a8be125a40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "269fb630-a40e-5592-b4e3-251a076c8d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-17782" :question/problem "![](images/ef/efc577f386353b4120dec733e5e6a627ad2412744dce4d71deba2b500a1edec2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-f(\\frac{1}{2}x-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=-f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-2))\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=-f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}x).$\n\n![](images/28/2891d009bd6f5d026ca47afe90f3ec4d6fafc654b7212392b325f76fe43f806e.png)\n\n \n\n- Then, translate the result by $2$ units to the right to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)).$\n\n![](images/ab/ab1f210741e01139618dc2fa1347cf1591cb90ca4f67906b89ebc7df29d77f32.png)\n\n- Finally, reflect the result across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)).$\n\n![](images/e5/e5552a14dc62407e02bb8f5e633ae194a95d313bce04abb3186b56ce83ec2974.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554562 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d948b64-117c-579e-b644-ff48d5689b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{7}=-\\frac{1}{2\\,187}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553899} {:answer/id #uuid "414e8dd7-f46d-5b69-b647-2ab2091b8a06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{7}=\\frac{1}{2\\,187}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553987} {:answer/id #uuid "66e2c5cc-c66f-5b95-bdf4-64edd8104ed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{7}=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554073} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b8e80e-0266-5cc0-9779-10e7d69bf61b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{7}=-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554231} {:answer/id #uuid "c88fe9b9-712f-5caa-8196-19c17631b170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{7}=2\\,187" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d948b64-117c-579e-b644-ff48d5689b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{7}=-\\frac{1}{2\\,187}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553899} :answer-field/id #uuid "baf33f80-cdc5-56ca-aedb-5cb060109b06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3805f218-eb22-5e05-bae4-085089844c85" :question/math-academy-id "q-67512" :question/problem "Find the equation for the common ratio $r$ of the geometric sequence whose $4$th term is $27$ and whose $11$th term is $-\\frac{1}{81}.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying this formula with $n=11$ and $n=4,$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{4-1}={a}_{1}{r}^{3}, \\\\ {a}_{11} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{11-1}={a}_{1}{r}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${a}_{4}=27$ and ${a}_{11}=-\\frac{1}{81},$ we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}{r}^{3}=27 \\\\ {a}_{1}{r}^{10}=-\\frac{1}{81}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by dividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{r}^{10}}{{a}_{1}{r}^{3}} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{81})}{27} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{\\,{a}_{1}\\,}{r}^{10}}{\\cancel{\\,{a}_{1}\\,}{r}^{3}} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{81})}{27} \\\\ \\frac{{r}^{10}}{{r}^{3}} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\,187} \\\\ {r}^{7} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\,187}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e72f358-8b3c-5fa0-a87f-b5c3e703b4d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553923} {:answer/id #uuid "22b7211e-dd6c-5a3e-88e8-d3edd2915633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553929} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1afbe3-f463-50af-b060-99529ce98d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554083} {:answer/id #uuid "909b2006-2b10-5c79-9d1c-af317380a62c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554182} {:answer/id #uuid "9c753113-557c-5b1c-8957-36be50789e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{4}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22b7211e-dd6c-5a3e-88e8-d3edd2915633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553929} :answer-field/id #uuid "5521173e-4fd0-569c-b113-b91da149682d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41a5c3bd-58e0-5924-89f4-127ab078d31e" :question/math-academy-id "q-141408" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=2{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}$ for $n\\ge 1$ \nconverge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence ${a}_{n}=2{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}$ is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{2}{3}.$ \n\nSince $|r|<1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554568 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14580d19-87db-5577-b886-dede88420678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553909} {:answer/id #uuid "1d5b4088-b923-5d8d-b898-9ef038b7221e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{20}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553921} {:answer/id #uuid "78790129-6ad3-5b94-a1fd-e8e6c328e78d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554111} {:answer/id #uuid "9212d90c-2509-5164-b39b-daaf88c41146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554190} {:answer/id #uuid "f407d38e-a26b-5b5c-8e63-0d3d2b384765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d5b4088-b923-5d8d-b898-9ef038b7221e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{20}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553921} :answer-field/id #uuid "5096af22-5b72-55f8-89a3-f7336942ca60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4220ee9a-71ec-56c9-a4ec-d0480b90a44d" :question/math-academy-id "q-53199" :question/problem "![](images/06/06f965498f6415c08b32c5ae30235a8eec65d6dd67cd1f349e80bcbe88bd5495.png)\n\nA boy $B$ is flying a kite $K$ with a $20\\,\\text{m}$ length of string. If the kite is rising vertically at a constant speed of $1\\,\\text{m/s},$ at what rate is the angle of elevation $\\theta $ increasing when $\\theta ={45}^{∘}?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/23/236144ff93bd22f5c4460c31e20216c45d6f8ac85dc945321dadecc093f379e0.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the kite's height, and $z$ is the distance between the boy and the kite. The quantity $z=20$ meters does not change with time because the string's length doesn't change.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{20} \\\\ y & =20\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(20\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(20\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConverting the given information from degrees to radians, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta ={45}^{∘}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{180}^{∘}}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =(20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{20} & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{20}$ radians per second."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554574 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7703407d-8788-52d9-8d23-7c0dd469068b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554108} {:answer/id #uuid "a7281cf1-df65-5001-bdd3-ab6876280750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554239} {:answer/id #uuid "bd204788-03a2-5b3d-ac8e-82ebffa05caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554278} {:answer/id #uuid "c4208108-7b91-5e37-9f02-765baf72fbe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554296} {:answer/id #uuid "e264fdd1-c875-5ee0-914c-54600fb544c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7281cf1-df65-5001-bdd3-ab6876280750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554239} :answer-field/id #uuid "5932e5d0-9d0c-54be-9a78-8ee0938071e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5964ffcf-bc61-5cdb-8ce3-fe3d629e8c05" :question/math-academy-id "q-340513" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nII. $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nIII. $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $2\\pi $-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+2\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $2\\pi $-periodic, because for any $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $2\\pi $-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80386a1f-1c68-542d-97ab-e6ec02fe80f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt[3]{(x+2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554125} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb339eb-1cc4-5d35-9e83-6c06760cd1fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{(3x+6)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554219} {:answer/id #uuid "c88b7035-072c-5214-bd55-1804a6aa0ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{(3x+6)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554310} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ae8cb1-1ad1-519a-a9ae-fc0f9b33b01f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{3x+6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554361} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4a59e0-949a-5cc8-91d2-09ecb393925e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt[3]{(x+2)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c88b7035-072c-5214-bd55-1804a6aa0ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{(3x+6)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554310} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c497ffd-4cca-50b5-a7fc-dc93d720ea25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c789989-28b9-52e0-ac72-a0f8b7d66a60" :question/math-academy-id "q-138860" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)(11{x}^{2}+24x-2)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}}{\\sqrt[3]{3x+6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6/3}}{(3x+6)^{1/3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{2}}{(3x+6)^{1/3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{2}}{(3x+6)^{1/3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2)^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((3x+6)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+6)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2}\\cdot 2x-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3x+6}\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+2}-\\frac{1}{3x+6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+2}-\\frac{1}{3x+6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}}(\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+2}-\\frac{1}{3x+6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}}(\\frac{4x(3x+6)-({x}^{2}+2)}{({x}^{2}+2)(3x+6)}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}}(\\frac{11{x}^{2}+24x-2}{({x}^{2}+2)(3x+6)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)(11{x}^{2}+24x-2)}{\\sqrt[3]{(3x-6)^{4}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\sqrt[3]{(3x-6)^{4}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554588 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4748909d-6528-5bed-ac92-aa61a9379c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554004} {:answer/id #uuid "4d1289eb-910a-5785-b6b0-3fd7998b0a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554018} {:answer/id #uuid "5685f6f8-da97-57f5-82fe-f48a3267d960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554037} {:answer/id #uuid "a499d257-2f0a-5c4a-b368-feeb1f43f5c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554230} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d91c8e-478d-51eb-a6d6-2618550a13c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a499d257-2f0a-5c4a-b368-feeb1f43f5c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554230} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e4ad903-4e09-5e2b-9085-556ef9945de6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7794f339-0fd4-50d9-a0ab-1ce4d82318cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-64032" :question/problem "Find the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose second term is $48$ and whose fifth term is $6.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms ${a}_{m}$ and ${a}_{n},$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given ${a}_{5}=6$ and ${a}_{2}=48.$ Substituting $n=5$ and $m=2$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{5-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{6}{48} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by solving the equation for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554601 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56ac1be1-fce4-5b14-a773-c0df14c2a9b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554039} {:answer/id #uuid "6417f28a-8f7f-5ecc-be6c-7c8da2415e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554064} {:answer/id #uuid "8301edff-e58a-593a-be70-3f68ef33e5f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554133} {:answer/id #uuid "8435d0e8-40cd-5cd4-89d6-083368872674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554139} {:answer/id #uuid "8f866f3c-7689-5311-b7e1-0b559c9e9370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8435d0e8-40cd-5cd4-89d6-083368872674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554139} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbf8a36f-7f35-538f-b598-e5154c361dd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f0ee2b9-8191-5f49-b4c7-7195eb067ecb" :question/math-academy-id "q-141404" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1}$ for $n\\ge 1$ \nconverge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence ${a}_{n}=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1}$ is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nSince $|r|<1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554602 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00514e84-3144-5df4-82c2-33eb39ce62b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553865} {:answer/id #uuid "8db89be6-e547-5fa5-9888-b55fe82c1cef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}(4-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554170} {:answer/id #uuid "9273e530-04e8-5996-b68a-1f42eb0f608e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}(2-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554192} {:answer/id #uuid "e88ae27f-5535-584b-9730-b1ae58287e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}(8-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554372} {:answer/id #uuid "e892a90b-d1ba-521a-b1de-129de62c0978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}(4-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e892a90b-d1ba-521a-b1de-129de62c0978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}(4-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554373} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5e8aa5a-ac76-5fe7-8cbb-d1e2111d6174" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8fe28743-a486-54a9-8fdd-bcbf858bcbbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-139035" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2{e}^{t/2},\\,y={e}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t/2})={e}^{t/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{t/2})}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}(1+{e}^{t})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{t/2}\\cdot {e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+{e}^{t}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}({4}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}(8-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}(4-\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172dbd89-674a-5726-9630-7750a809157b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553913} {:answer/id #uuid "a6329dad-6c6c-5def-8fa9-b82fbf186adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554235} {:answer/id #uuid "cb86a186-51eb-54d5-a27b-6e2065c66032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554318} {:answer/id #uuid "f4430166-3747-5209-944a-ba1d986e72e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554397} {:answer/id #uuid "fae193f4-9c23-5527-acbb-523664fd50e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fae193f4-9c23-5527-acbb-523664fd50e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554409} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdd707aa-f658-5dc0-88e8-2cc1aff0bd8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5c77383-51aa-56e3-a7b4-492c5c358798" :question/math-academy-id "q-64033" :question/problem "Find the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose fourth term is $135$ and whose seventh term is $5.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms ${a}_{m}$ and ${a}_{n},$ we use the formula \n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given ${a}_{7}=5$ and ${a}_{4}=135.$ Substituting $n=7$ and $m=4$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{7-4} & =\\frac{{a}_{7}}{{a}_{4}} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{5}{135} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by solving the equation for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0acd652e-3691-5e84-bd19-a5ecf963137a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0acd652e-3691-5e84-bd19-a5ecf963137a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553891} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1bfd8d1-d406-5259-8076-e675c9f53bf2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d00df18b-1c5e-50a7-89f5-e1bc96838901" :question/math-academy-id "q-314883" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7de8ecd3d178cf07377aef5ca96ce28f638700e6284d151e3578ba11ed87327a.png)\n\nEthan notices a drone descending. He estimates that the distance between his location and the point on the ground directly beneath the drone is $150\\,\\text{ft}.$ If the angle of elevation $\\theta $ between him and the drone decreases at a rate of $0.1\\,\\text{rad/s},$ at what rate is its altitude decreasing when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}?$ Assume that the rate of decreasing altitude is **positive** and do not round the answer.\n\nThe drone's altitude is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{ft/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/50/5024a642ac27698d848577165b08e3158d3248d50c064a79432fcad6e2df90d5.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the drone’s altitude, and $x$ is the distance between Ethan and the shadow. The quantity $x=150\\,\\text{ft}$ does not change with time because the distance between Ethan and the shadow is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{150} \\\\ y & =150\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(150\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =150{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-0.1$ (negative because the angle is decreasing) and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =150{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})(-0.1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =150(4)(-0.1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone's altitude is decreasing at a rate of $60\\,\\text{ft/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554643 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c9dab7-2e99-5365-b6f9-2f92a770a384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0e/0e49310e5441552232d841954e95fe3b38015b27565050fbd384d54b35a56bfc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553883} {:answer/id #uuid "11d75bbb-77b6-5e55-baef-b5f3c412881a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8f/8f8f679798bf48d8543522dfc3c77c25de4ebfd55618c17ea033549caea2f40a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553906} {:answer/id #uuid "56a351e4-0067-508f-9e5a-e7e8908908cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3d4a416592d9fe3683f6b39bb99ebef44d3f0b36c37b50f86c9dac5fa8aa862f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554038} {:answer/id #uuid "89c6681c-3b74-5712-ac28-f5586469ed52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d2/d2dad7c3e898a680e07eba09d822733619f1158f9877136ba11078e90cbf75c2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554159} {:answer/id #uuid "e204c221-f408-5b2f-b387-49819b320673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/eb85e0857a24ae273e7e768c51e80c08461be095e502e45c2c83e7340bf725b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06c9dab7-2e99-5365-b6f9-2f92a770a384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0e/0e49310e5441552232d841954e95fe3b38015b27565050fbd384d54b35a56bfc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553883} :answer-field/id #uuid "16b058af-5b74-55ea-8310-41c6fb4502e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f73ff2c0-b98a-5833-8e43-d855656798f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-114069" :question/problem "![](images/93/938babc27340209a1a5477fb3184abbf155f409c520c76995c4e234f99691347.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-f(\\frac{x}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=-f(\\frac{x}{3}),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=3$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{x}{3}).$\n\n![](images/f0/f0b289d42620a275eeb90d4bdbe265ca92ea4264de70ecc11e5f661a84149628.png)\n\n- Then, reflect the result across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-f(\\frac{x}{3}).$\n\n![](images/59/597ba19a0c993ea597ecd091df02d97e974364125c79780df04aef365442abb6.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554837 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9b48b0-d97f-58e4-9020-b27a2d4f3d6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{8}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554819} {:answer/id #uuid "8bb04077-d22e-5117-b194-0f0acf1d352d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{30}+2\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{80}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554831} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5683dd-3b44-569f-9184-b9a9dd913da0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\frac{\\sqrt{30}-2\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{80}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554832} {:answer/id #uuid "a38f036c-5867-564b-8d07-e1b6b83b524f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{8}}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554834} {:answer/id #uuid "e97936bc-ddbb-5b82-994c-9bbad040900c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{30}-2\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{80}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e97936bc-ddbb-5b82-994c-9bbad040900c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{30}-2\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{80}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554847} :answer-field/id #uuid "db09d6ae-d98a-5518-8809-069b4c1aee1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b3e4b28c-e8a8-5cbb-89ef-d351d2bc4c04" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨-\\sqrt{3},-\\sqrt{6},1⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,1,\\sqrt{6}⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\sqrt{3})(-1)+(-\\sqrt{6})(1)+(1)(\\sqrt{6})}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{6})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{8}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{b\\cdot a}{|\\,a\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{b}_{x}{a}_{x}+{b}_{y}{a}_{y}+{b}_{z}{a}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(-\\sqrt{6})^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{8}}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{30}-2\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{80}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3008eecb-dd1f-56ce-9019-7b10e3694893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554822} {:answer/id #uuid "3dbe5db7-75f9-5a77-b537-b1a09cc57e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{10}}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554824} {:answer/id #uuid "46374ed0-5dc9-5f91-a8d1-bd7992f47ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}-4\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{200}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554826} {:answer/id #uuid "d309d8da-6af7-57bb-b7ea-1d4bce7cb08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}+4\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{200}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554840} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b4caf3-f064-58e4-810d-38b21051f146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}+4\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{200}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d309d8da-6af7-57bb-b7ea-1d4bce7cb08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}+4\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{200}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554840} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab1bd42b-9b4a-500f-a3fe-01ffe62acb76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d2188174-52cd-511b-87a0-828e4b888f06" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3},4⟩$ and $b=⟨-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{6},\\sqrt{2}⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a+{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)(-\\sqrt{2})+(-\\sqrt{3})(\\sqrt{6})+(4)(\\sqrt{2})}{\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(\\sqrt{6})^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{2}+4\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{b\\cdot a}{|\\,a\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{b}_{x}{a}_{x}+{b}_{y}{a}_{y}+{b}_{z}{a}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{20}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a+{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{10}}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}+4\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{200}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554842 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2256bc8f-16a5-5763-9ff1-4dffbeae3f17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-\\sqrt{15}-1}{\\sqrt{8}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554821} {:answer/id #uuid "31ba76c7-5b61-52e2-8626-7c128445a699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}\\left(-\\sqrt{15}-1\\right)-(2\\sqrt{2}\\left(-\\sqrt{15}-1\\right))}{\\sqrt{120}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554823} {:answer/id #uuid "c624c424-e407-59d8-b6d8-5d60cf147b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}\\left(-\\sqrt{15}-1\\right)+(2\\sqrt{2}\\left(-\\sqrt{15}-1\\right))}{\\sqrt{120}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554838} {:answer/id #uuid "d311c211-ba0b-574c-b6f7-1faff00bc1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\frac{\\sqrt{15}\\left(-\\sqrt{15}-1\\right)-(2\\sqrt{2}\\left(-\\sqrt{15}-1\\right))}{\\sqrt{120}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554841} {:answer/id #uuid "ef54b90c-8daf-5b9c-bdc2-f56a5c5c5ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-\\sqrt{15}-1}{\\sqrt{8}}-(\\frac{-\\sqrt{15}-1}{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31ba76c7-5b61-52e2-8626-7c128445a699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}\\left(-\\sqrt{15}-1\\right)-(2\\sqrt{2}\\left(-\\sqrt{15}-1\\right))}{\\sqrt{120}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554823} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f521ab9-1ddd-5450-85ba-9420283060bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d9750954-2644-5f46-9bc3-7cd5fcb4219c" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨3,\\sqrt{5},1⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3},2⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)(-1)+(\\sqrt{5})(-\\sqrt{3})+(1)(2)}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-\\sqrt{15}+2}{\\sqrt{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\sqrt{15}-1}{\\sqrt{8}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{b\\cdot a}{|\\,a\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{b}_{x}{a}_{x}+{b}_{y}{a}_{y}+{b}_{z}{a}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\sqrt{15}-1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\sqrt{15}-1}{\\sqrt{15}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =-\\frac{1+\\sqrt{15}}{\\sqrt{8}}-(-\\frac{1+\\sqrt{15}}{\\sqrt{15}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}\\left(-\\sqrt{15}-1\\right)-(2\\sqrt{2}\\left(-\\sqrt{15}-1\\right))}{\\sqrt{120}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fea03f7-25d3-517e-9cc2-81572e335e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}\\left(-6-3\\sqrt{3}\\right)+(\\sqrt{31}\\left(-6-3\\sqrt{3}\\right))}{\\sqrt{558}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554825} {:answer/id #uuid "5ef9a9d5-a739-56b2-9661-ac1056cf1fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-6-3\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{31}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554829} {:answer/id #uuid "7531ab09-81e4-58af-afd3-fb322d27c79f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}\\left(-6-3\\sqrt{3}\\right)-(\\sqrt{31}\\left(-6-3\\sqrt{3}\\right))}{\\sqrt{558}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554830} {:answer/id #uuid "a18083e1-382f-5d93-afe5-ff52c2b3cc00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}\\left(-6-3\\sqrt{3}\\right)-(\\sqrt{31}\\left(-6-3\\sqrt{3}\\right))}{\\sqrt{558}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554833} {:answer/id #uuid "e10defb3-8236-5ae1-b347-1f178892fd43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-6-3\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{31}}-(\\frac{-6-3\\sqrt{3}}{18})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7531ab09-81e4-58af-afd3-fb322d27c79f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}\\left(-6-3\\sqrt{3}\\right)-(\\sqrt{31}\\left(-6-3\\sqrt{3}\\right))}{\\sqrt{558}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554830} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f3ed2bb-7c5c-5d4f-8584-c6f1cbf01e87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "de71841a-f8e7-51cc-a207-4dfd1f658c74" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨\\sqrt{3},3,-\\sqrt{6}⟩$ and $b=⟨-3,-4,-\\sqrt{6}⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\sqrt{3})(-3)+(3)(-4)+(-\\sqrt{6})(-\\sqrt{6})}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-\\sqrt{6})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}-12+6}{\\sqrt{31}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6-3\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{31}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{b\\cdot a}{|\\,a\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{b}_{x}{a}_{x}+{b}_{y}{a}_{y}+{b}_{z}{a}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6-3\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{6})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6-3\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{18}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}+6}{\\sqrt{31}}-(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}+6}{\\sqrt{18}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}\\left(-6-3\\sqrt{3}\\right)-(\\sqrt{31}\\left(-6-3\\sqrt{3}\\right))}{\\sqrt{558}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a869629-3129-5a48-b814-3ca9009f93a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and radius is $75$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554929} {:answer/id #uuid "5363a872-00c2-59dc-9aef-c1861b33c441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2})$ and radius is $5 \\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554933} {:answer/id #uuid "694d2ebd-518e-56b2-91e5-b162c47dfcdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{1}{2})$ and radius is $5 \\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554942} {:answer/id #uuid "7039e8d1-5a0b-5709-9a54-16e8324253c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and radius is $5 \\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554945} {:answer/id #uuid "7a590015-345d-55cf-902a-d501711e512c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and radius is $5 \\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7039e8d1-5a0b-5709-9a54-16e8324253c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and radius is $5 \\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554945} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5c87030-ce63-5b8f-a2fb-928bbcbfc368" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61a667fa-1c17-504b-b910-d39cb45f95f5" :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x+\\frac{3}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2}=75?$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x+\\frac{3}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2} & =75 \\\\ (x-(- \\frac{3}{2}))^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2} & =(5 \\sqrt{3})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius is the positive square root of the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{75}=\\sqrt{25\\cdot 3}=5 \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=- \\frac{3}{2},$ $b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and $r=5 \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and its radius is $5 \\sqrt{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14bf130e-5183-5004-9744-fe22eba9aa52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(- \\frac{9}{2},\\frac{1}{3})$ and radius is $2 \\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554921} {:answer/id #uuid "5a3b6c09-e3df-5465-b189-6b05b4e444b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(- \\frac{9}{2},- \\frac{1}{3})$ and radius is $2 \\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554934} {:answer/id #uuid "5c664509-2dac-5ad9-95fa-1d73576ad5c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(- \\frac{9}{2},- \\frac{1}{3})$ and radius is $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554937} {:answer/id #uuid "c54cdb2d-ed97-56f8-bc85-c3ce69c7f983" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\frac{9}{2},- \\frac{1}{3})$ and radius is $2 \\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554968} {:answer/id #uuid "ec7cfa1e-3234-5e46-afc4-96a4b57d942b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\frac{9}{2},\\frac{1}{3})$ and radius is $2 \\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a3b6c09-e3df-5465-b189-6b05b4e444b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(- \\frac{9}{2},- \\frac{1}{3})$ and radius is $2 \\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554934} :answer-field/id #uuid "f96f4672-1e36-5de0-9612-5b0f7789c726" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "622d38f7-0ff2-5ba3-916c-fd8be20f31cd" :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x+\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{1}{3})^{2}=12?$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x+\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{1}{3})^{2} & =12 \\\\ (x-(- \\frac{9}{2}))^{2}+(y-(- \\frac{1}{3}))^{2} & =(2 \\sqrt{3})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius is the positive square root of the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{12}=\\sqrt{4\\cdot 3}=2 \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=- \\frac{9}{2},$ $b=- \\frac{1}{3},$ and $r=2 \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(- \\frac{9}{2},- \\frac{1}{3})$ and its radius is $2 \\sqrt{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "112cf927-0220-54f4-a54e-025b846b3990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(- \\frac{9}{2},1)$ and radius is $\\sqrt{7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554920} {:answer/id #uuid "b67bc386-aa35-511c-9c25-2b8fcd11882e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\frac{9}{2},-1)$ and radius is $\\sqrt{7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554965} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e33098-66e1-5b5f-88b7-0fbd06a4983f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(- \\frac{9}{2},1)$ and radius is $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554966} {:answer/id #uuid "c696482b-c7fa-5e91-a0bd-2cac3b4af572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\frac{9}{2},1)$ and radius is $\\sqrt{7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554970} {:answer/id #uuid "f55cace1-0447-5da2-a569-223e42c722dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(- \\frac{9}{2},-1)$ and radius is $\\sqrt{7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "112cf927-0220-54f4-a54e-025b846b3990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(- \\frac{9}{2},1)$ and radius is $\\sqrt{7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554920} :answer-field/id #uuid "94365b25-472f-5909-a88b-754bcce9e2ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ac782b7-be87-511e-a388-46f13109d6ce" :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x+\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=7?$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x+\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =7 \\\\ (x-(- \\frac{9}{2}))^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{7})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=- \\frac{9}{2},$ $b=1,$ and $r=\\sqrt{7}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(- \\frac{9}{2},1)$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554947 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05deb54f-1278-5d90-a4df-cb11ac5345fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(3,- \\frac{3}{2})$ and radius is $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554916} {:answer/id #uuid "0f911ec9-43c1-57c5-8bc3-386823389a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(3,\\frac{3}{2})$ and radius is $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554919} {:answer/id #uuid "1cd20098-d6fa-5aac-a27d-dfb6dd53ec8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(-3,- \\frac{3}{2})$ and radius is $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554925} {:answer/id #uuid "4f47e046-6d0e-59a0-9491-42bc567168bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(3,- \\frac{3}{2})$ and radius is $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554931} {:answer/id #uuid "e326265a-ef5b-5fa5-a1d8-70164b421f43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(-3,\\frac{3}{2})$ and radius is $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05deb54f-1278-5d90-a4df-cb11ac5345fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(3,- \\frac{3}{2})$ and radius is $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554916} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a6a6ce4-7edc-5db9-9755-1bc6927c7c7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73554832-d205-5c91-af0d-110fdbd01485" :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x-3)^{2}+(y+\\frac{3}{2})^{2}=6?$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y+\\frac{3}{2})^{2} & =6 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-(- \\frac{3}{2}))^{2} & =(\\sqrt{6})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=3,$ $b=- \\frac{3}{2},$ and $r=\\sqrt{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(3,- \\frac{3}{2})$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41eb3881-27fe-5aa3-9e5e-d8094356821e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\frac{5}{3},\\frac{7}{2})$ and radius is $6 \\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554928} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb35869-a632-5bb5-bc7a-4c4f84e3cca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(- \\frac{5}{3},- \\frac{7}{2})$ and radius is $6 \\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554959} {:answer/id #uuid "ca282382-0037-5a62-9b81-40739d2aa75f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(- \\frac{5}{3},\\frac{7}{2})$ and radius is $6 \\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554972} {:answer/id #uuid "f1954577-1420-5f95-b0fd-4b51291d3078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\frac{5}{3},- \\frac{7}{2})$ and radius is $6 \\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554979} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9211b3-e46e-5cf6-b678-fafa2ce94c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\frac{5}{3},- \\frac{7}{2})$ and radius is $72$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1954577-1420-5f95-b0fd-4b51291d3078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(\\frac{5}{3},- \\frac{7}{2})$ and radius is $6 \\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554979} :answer-field/id #uuid "c699ae23-4ea3-52d8-bcad-f2bd30023082" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "915cc9d8-786e-5928-932b-d3b73bca3d09" :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x-\\frac{5}{3})^{2}+(y+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}=72?$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{5}{3})^{2}+(y+\\frac{7}{2})^{2} & =72 \\\\ (x-\\frac{5}{3})^{2}+(y-(- \\frac{7}{2}))^{2} & =(6 \\sqrt{2})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius is the positive square root of the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{72}=\\sqrt{36\\cdot 2}=6 \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=\\frac{5}{3},$ $b=- \\frac{7}{2},$ and $r=6 \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(\\frac{5}{3},- \\frac{7}{2})$ and its radius is $6 \\sqrt{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555764 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7cd4b8-d810-5af2-844a-029c0614f602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555029} {:answer/id #uuid "10021868-bebf-5d55-88d6-1666034c1a46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{(1-y)/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555031} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef13b49-b8a1-573d-960c-ebf7e8cd471e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{(1-y)/2}, & y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555056} {:answer/id #uuid "df0e68c0-8aa4-5c57-a44f-b03b70691336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{1-y/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555543} {:answer/id #uuid "f4bcd3f9-a718-5576-984c-731f9180bd20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y/2}, & y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f7cd4b8-d810-5af2-844a-029c0614f602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555029} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ea4d1f3-3230-5c90-8f5f-f708753efce4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10780b62-18db-53fc-94bd-63d320bb4c73" :question/math-academy-id "q-143774" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-x},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=2X,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=2X.$ The transformation $u(x)=2x$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =2y \\\\ y & =2{u}^{-1}(y) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\frac{y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2})\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}| \\\\  & ={e}^{1-y/2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $x\\ge 1,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }u(x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1-y/2}, & y\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25d6bf9d-c36b-57f4-a4a3-d6d10d20a9fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555078} {:answer/id #uuid "2d113435-7d4d-5e64-bf7a-6096265af4ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555100} {:answer/id #uuid "97a99f95-7b5c-511f-b1dd-63628675fb7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555361} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5ab758-9dd9-5005-99ab-90aa7d7d9c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555414} {:answer/id #uuid "b15e8623-fcd9-53a6-8695-28d9aee82949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac5ab758-9dd9-5005-99ab-90aa7d7d9c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555414} :answer-field/id #uuid "89806148-2b04-5dff-9c9d-9aecabad8125" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1cf3361e-986e-5750-bf9f-73762dff3b0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-106771" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{1}{2},$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20e4a15a-eda1-52a1-8baa-9cdce6bed32e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555063} {:answer/id #uuid "2718af2e-0e58-5f94-b179-4b6122d8bbc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555083} {:answer/id #uuid "84737793-7f74-5db9-b6ff-72f64548ee3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555306} {:answer/id #uuid "959f0371-d54d-54ee-88dc-a7c52e86bcd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555355} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f0f01f-aa87-552e-8444-03f833352b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2718af2e-0e58-5f94-b179-4b6122d8bbc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555083} :answer-field/id #uuid "8abcfe54-e8c7-5c1d-9465-cff1e4813088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "206046fc-72eb-5e7e-8330-c7dfc01fc134" :question/math-academy-id "q-84976" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${y}^{2}=-2(x-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n(y-0)^{2}=4(-\\frac{2}{4})(x-1),\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n(y-0)^{2}=4(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-1).\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=1$ and $k=0.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(1,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555781 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47616c55-81d5-53dd-a98b-37ede48b1258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555152} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4c4b5a-2d3b-5374-9935-fdd0f3bad0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{4{x}^{3}}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555447} {:answer/id #uuid "db526ff7-5e00-5117-89dd-9ef35a7b067c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555532} {:answer/id #uuid "df40addd-09f3-5272-8d1e-ee10182960a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2(x-2)^{3}}-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555545} {:answer/id #uuid "fce357ef-4288-5d72-8d01-538c32549ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{(x-2)^{3}}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db526ff7-5e00-5117-89dd-9ef35a7b067c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555532} :answer-field/id #uuid "42914f58-aa47-51a0-8fbf-7284670805d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "300089ff-7a76-5b21-82d9-6ccfc2e0a8c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-148557" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2x-3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2x-3}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2y-3}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2y-3}} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2y-3} & =\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{2y-3})}^{3} & ={(\\frac{2}{x})}^{3} \\\\ 2y-3 & =\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ 2y & =\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{3}{2},\\,f(x)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne \\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555788 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bdc0aa2-9e6d-5049-b072-5b10305b8745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{64(x-5)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555096} {:answer/id #uuid "4f82c30e-c8c0-5715-961f-5c2888701119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{64}{9}(x-5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555178} {:answer/id #uuid "63a118d0-973e-503f-9dae-703dd3bf2be1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{64}(x-5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555223} {:answer/id #uuid "8942b6cd-3961-531f-a8ba-bff58e7ddaaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3}{8(x-5)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555319} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c318c9-9d14-5ca4-90e0-ce7b4d05d8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{8}{3(x-5)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bdc0aa2-9e6d-5049-b072-5b10305b8745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{64(x-5)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555096} :answer-field/id #uuid "23b3eabf-5eed-56de-9552-c3437f08c08d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "374308b9-fd72-5948-9d04-e71abbe2f03a" :question/math-academy-id "q-148745" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=5-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=5-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=5-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{y}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ x-5 & =-\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =-\\frac{3}{8(x-5)} \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(-\\frac{3}{8(x-5)})}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{64(x-5)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{64(x-5)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)<5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx<5,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39552913-1c07-56b5-b6b4-c97f61c68646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555123} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9eedfb-df31-5fef-89f8-4a0a5bc39de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{4{x}^{4}z}{y}+6{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555177} {:answer/id #uuid "738b92d9-e5fc-511d-904f-c37c0cfe4696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}z}{y}+3x{z}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555259} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e6de29-d31f-58b5-b882-4e6e62b0a4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}z}{y}+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555389} {:answer/id #uuid "e5810984-6602-54b8-9000-97b31b9afc00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-4{x}^{4}z+4{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39552913-1c07-56b5-b6b4-c97f61c68646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555123} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d1ececc-599e-58e1-a6af-f6ea1d9aa196" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5386af7a-f43c-5f81-bd4e-2f8f31eb31ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-110680" :question/problem "Find, in ascending powers of $z$ and descending powers of $x,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}-z{y}^{2})}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},$ and $b=-z{y}^{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}-z{y}^{2})}^{3} & =1{a}^{3}{b}^{0}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{a}^{0}{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{3}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{0}+3{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{2}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{1} \\\\  & \\,+3{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{1}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{2}+1{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{0}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =1(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}})(1)+3(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}})(-z{y}^{2}) \\\\  & \\,+3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})({z}^{2}{y}^{4})+1(1)(-{z}^{3}{y}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{4}}{y}-{z}^{3}{y}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}-{z}^{3}{y}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind, in ascending powers of $z$ and descending powers of $x,$ the first three terms are\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54b20548-2edc-51a1-b679-8f447c0e500a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{8(x+3)^3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54b20548-2edc-51a1-b679-8f447c0e500a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{8(x+3)^3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555189} :answer-field/id #uuid "75df9ea4-ee8b-50c0-8c6b-c1147f92edcc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "672f62ec-04ae-5b96-b078-6b34b51f6cb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-270765" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{x}}-3.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{x}}-3\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{y}}-3.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{y}}-3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2\\sqrt[3]{y}} & =x+3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{5}{2(x+3)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{3} & ={(\\frac{5}{2(x+3)})}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{125}{8(x+3)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{125}{8(x+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,f(x)\\ne -3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne -3,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555826 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17ce7ecd-d0ed-5840-93f3-0001b823057c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555045} {:answer/id #uuid "32707e5b-5823-5a1d-bd2b-ef75cdb2b376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555107} {:answer/id #uuid "760a156c-e362-5d1a-b5ea-f2896456664c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555267} {:answer/id #uuid "c9edd21a-02c0-5f4e-9d8c-e390fbf70430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555487} {:answer/id #uuid "ed4e4c09-aee9-5012-986e-68aab485dedf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9edd21a-02c0-5f4e-9d8c-e390fbf70430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555487} :answer-field/id #uuid "20a9e958-7605-5553-8817-a7378b83215d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8015d6e0-b5ed-541d-9586-128e40ee9b69" :question/math-academy-id "q-33799" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bf8bd7c7679e1c1df6c336b48892964c7658ac877b5efc22b099b1e98129d9ac.png)\n\nWhat is the radius of a sphere whose volume is $\\frac{343}{6}\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $V=\\frac{343}{6}\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{343}{6}\\pi  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{343\\pi \\cdot 3}{6\\cdot 4\\pi } & ={r}^{3} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{343}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{7}^{3}}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{7}{2})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{3}}=\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b92942c-8aba-5aa1-868e-af6ff0b747b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555050} {:answer/id #uuid "6332f8e1-70e3-52dc-ba10-2ad158c200d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555219} {:answer/id #uuid "6de35bfe-a94a-5f6f-a2a7-be882652597a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555251} {:answer/id #uuid "98125233-946f-554a-8efb-6031235b27b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555364} {:answer/id #uuid "f833dfc3-dd27-5cc6-a187-eb5f1348d40e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b92942c-8aba-5aa1-868e-af6ff0b747b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555050} :answer-field/id #uuid "eada811c-d053-5120-a961-fe8ffd045312" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b1a0e36-dce4-509e-90b0-4f68fe84ad7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-84968" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(x+5)^{2}=-3(y-6)?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{3}{4})(y-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=-5$ and $k=6.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(-5,6).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555842 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d5889ff-4fef-5a31-a5a9-2dbffb765ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555025} {:answer/id #uuid "62d815c5-e833-5b48-87e0-0830919fd657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555218} {:answer/id #uuid "a010143e-ec2c-5360-b6bf-45e949bd9bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555386} {:answer/id #uuid "d918cea8-7679-5409-902e-13d46623dee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555524} {:answer/id #uuid "e42b10c9-8668-50fd-9c16-8cf7778d16eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d5889ff-4fef-5a31-a5a9-2dbffb765ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555025} :answer-field/id #uuid "02aa06e3-c429-50fd-98e7-14828dfd8707" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a102024c-363c-5adb-a524-cd390e67faf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-148573" :question/problem "The function $f(x),$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{x+k}}+5,\\,x>-k\n$$\n\nhas an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{9(x-5)^{2}}-2,\\,x>5.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{x+k}}+5\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{y+k}}+5.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{y+k}}+5 \\\\ x-5 & =\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{y+k}} \\\\ \\sqrt{y+k} & =\\frac{2}{3(x-5)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y+k})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{2}{3(x-5)})}^{2} \\\\ y+k & =\\frac{4}{9(x-5)^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{9(x-5)^{2}}-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{9(x-5)^{2}}-k,\\,x>5.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\nk=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d9e3163-77a3-5b77-b6dd-5f9cc4183e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555102} {:answer/id #uuid "4aaceaf8-31cb-57d9-89f4-40f6fbb0f71e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555164} {:answer/id #uuid "8720512d-ed7f-54db-9220-6ac8c52ec59f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555313} {:answer/id #uuid "d7eb0642-fe00-5e0f-a9ab-9dcbf769b12b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555520} {:answer/id #uuid "f60b95e6-696c-5f40-9e72-4690b1e4355c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f60b95e6-696c-5f40-9e72-4690b1e4355c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555611} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9f334df-9edf-56f6-b904-ca6fc39afbf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c47ad7a3-3c57-54bc-8711-6eebfda1e26f" :question/math-academy-id "q-30452" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=4{x}^{2}-2x+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=4$ and $b=-2$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{-2}{2(4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{-2}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4{x}^{2}-2x+1 \\\\  & =4{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-2(\\frac{1}{4})+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{2}{4}+\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555854 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0824024b-c099-527a-8256-d5a6127f2786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555013} {:answer/id #uuid "2f22d275-c75b-5b64-9666-30863a46ed54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{15}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555104} {:answer/id #uuid "4087805a-ed7e-522b-a9a2-c73758870563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555140} {:answer/id #uuid "76e3d3fa-3854-5450-9938-38191ec8e7d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{35}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555274} {:answer/id #uuid "f412a256-5361-5b16-a302-db1d43b422fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4087805a-ed7e-522b-a9a2-c73758870563" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555140} :answer-field/id #uuid "15067fae-69bc-5d5f-83e8-e1ebdc618e5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4e9e89a-1628-511a-8a61-3e91affcd33c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106134" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8cd1c9ab91a23fae12dd45078ec44ca55e69da8aa787775d7a9583857a84cb71.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a parabola. The $x$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$ are $\\frac{11}{6}$ and $\\frac{25}{6},$ respectively.  Find the axis of symmetry of the parabola." :question/worked-solution "We're given that the roots of the parabola are \n ${x}_{1}=\\frac{11}{6}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{25}{6}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{11}{6}+\\frac{25}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{36}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08fdd0c6-1d98-54af-82c9-a3de113fe661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555016} {:answer/id #uuid "0ee61fd2-c684-57e7-9ea4-f09a8e349764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555027} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9c7297-d71f-5a15-82a4-809b7fbacfeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555379} {:answer/id #uuid "aa206b43-8b04-5867-a4d8-d7210015326b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555406} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7d4718-a5a3-5cee-ad89-6d539fb4ecc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb7d4718-a5a3-5cee-ad89-6d539fb4ecc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555488} :answer-field/id #uuid "45b92ccf-c96a-52fb-b618-ecc3a61419d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d36205bb-47f5-5c37-9085-d080474849b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-131169" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{30(x-1)}{{x}^{7}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the variance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute $\\text{E}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }x\\cdot \\frac{30(x-1)}{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{30x-30}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{30}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{30}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{30}{4{x}^{4}}+\\frac{30}{5{x}^{5}}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2{x}^{4}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{5}}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{30(x-1)}{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{30x-30}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{30}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{30}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{30}{3{x}^{3}}+\\frac{30}{4{x}^{4}}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{15}{2{x}^{4}}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "049d033e-9ea7-58bc-bb55-6b11185d1b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "049d033e-9ea7-58bc-bb55-6b11185d1b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556352} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eaa9ff4-4551-5f33-ba72-289eaa72b4fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0921e1f0-6016-5a9a-b00a-281e162349d5" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa571435e7a44532cfb3bc61965b56b10e83c090236349851971e1b51c007ade.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{11}{2}\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.$ Use $\\sin30^{\\circ}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, $AC=${{field-1}} $\\text{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=2\\text{ cm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{11}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{11}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{11}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{\\frac{11}{2}}{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ b & =11\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=11\\,\\text{cm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff2e4436-f636-5aa3-88e5-00bf7a733fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff2e4436-f636-5aa3-88e5-00bf7a733fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556405} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce102e6e-9d86-54c9-b424-61ab30bebf30" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1f90245f-9fc6-5863-a434-c42642a9ebf6" :question/problem "![](images/76/76bdcef8f267043fe1f9c97f989ff3d6bfdf3b071158501cf43aef78976e93eb.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{1247\\sqrt{2}}{64}\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.$ Then, $AC=${{field-1}} $\\text{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=\\frac{43}{4}\\text{ cm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{1247\\sqrt{2}}{64} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot \\frac{43}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{1247\\sqrt{2}}{64} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot \\frac{43}{4}\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1247\\sqrt{2}}{64} & =\\frac{43\\sqrt{2}}{16}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{\\frac{1247\\sqrt{2}}{64}}{\\frac{43\\sqrt{2}}{16}} \\\\ b & =\\frac{29}{4}\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=\\frac{29}{4}\\,\\text{cm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b8b15ed-493d-56f0-91b4-96c0546ae3a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556362} {:answer/id #uuid "8a3aafe4-37fc-512f-9335-c0b5740cbecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{7}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556375} {:answer/id #uuid "c2121115-e08d-5100-ac2e-6cef9261566c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556393} {:answer/id #uuid "e19dcdf1-d006-5161-a5fd-af6492cf1462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556398} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c66126-e596-580e-91de-79431398b6de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "294\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e19dcdf1-d006-5161-a5fd-af6492cf1462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556398} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba9f84d4-40bc-5670-b78b-e2d89edb52b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56e458c0-2e91-5258-b0ff-cd258f195c67" :question/problem "![](images/69/6949b198287b58d95ba47662a3432760d7e5a98d60bd006541269abcc9f3e2ab.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{21}{2}\\,\\text{mm}^{2}.$ Use $\\sin30^{\\circ}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find $AC.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=7\\text{ mm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{21}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 7\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{21}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{21}{2} & =\\frac{7}{4}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{\\frac{21}{2}}{\\frac{7}{4}} \\\\ b & =6\\text{ mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=6\\,\\text{mm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb0dc146-0b8f-5788-b4d6-1d2af2fc1489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb0dc146-0b8f-5788-b4d6-1d2af2fc1489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556404} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ea43d3b-18e5-535a-9041-c2927666f8ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93b23a78-a854-5cfb-a02e-a33896d85d60" :question/problem "![](images/52/52e6b64e78550f0e1cfeecc59a606921b52af85c457fff5e936f45a0a732c243.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $1\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.$ Use $\\sin30^{\\circ}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, $AC=${{field-1}} $\\text{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=2\\text{ cm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{2}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ b & =2\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=2\\,\\text{cm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556381 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d6f5fef-1f26-5927-9aba-3e07ba091909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "588\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556355} {:answer/id #uuid "1ddcbedc-9b6d-572c-a3ed-af91585959a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556358} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea08d46-2b55-577e-86f2-f830a33e8ccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556382} {:answer/id #uuid "a5562289-9847-5a37-82e2-0f14fe73c7e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{7}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556385} {:answer/id #uuid "ba510cf0-a3a0-5d06-8f32-bab53002d3b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba510cf0-a3a0-5d06-8f32-bab53002d3b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556389} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0ba1fed-347d-5277-b120-1a48f185cceb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9cd422c2-0775-5873-a783-f544ad93d54d" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f14b28ecb2ab34328e461e66b6c00c5516b99d559e675db06306f804d5aaf29.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $21\\,\\text{mm}^{2}.$ Use $\\sin30^{\\circ}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find $AC.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=7\\text{ mm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 21 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 7\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\ 21 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 21 & =\\frac{7}{4}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{21}{\\frac{7}{4}} \\\\ b & =12\\text{ mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=12\\,\\text{mm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556384 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9829a6-c87f-5189-a880-168c6d74d94d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556356} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6abb05-37ef-5299-9ef6-0a9a17779c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{4}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556372} {:answer/id #uuid "9837de89-45e1-5819-b1cc-f2b9b2eb134c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{700}{3}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556380} {:answer/id #uuid "d3aea810-d0d0-5240-9d22-b3038bdb5776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{8}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556395} {:answer/id #uuid "f893f514-4f5e-528a-8157-ca6cd7b413a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{63}{80}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d6abb05-37ef-5299-9ef6-0a9a17779c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{4}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556372} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba366e8a-53d8-5743-af27-e93d5394e3a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9f5682f6-556a-5e14-92ca-e2c5f602999e" :question/problem "![](images/89/89a4b38c29ef11835af24fa0b98ee45a7f5fcbc6ee0a5fd0a3de4d0096144659.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{35\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.$ Find $AC.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=\\frac{20}{3}\\text{ cm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={135}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{135}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{35\\sqrt{2}}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot \\frac{20}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{135}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{35\\sqrt{2}}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot \\frac{20}{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{35\\sqrt{2}}{4} & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{3}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{\\frac{35\\sqrt{2}}{4}}{\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{3}} \\\\ b & =\\frac{21}{4}\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=\\frac{21}{4}\\,\\text{cm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31587425-b010-55dd-9843-c96a4d64e79b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{3}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556360} {:answer/id #uuid "53db9127-7086-5ad7-9c28-d318c22bbce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5324}{27}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556364} {:answer/id #uuid "59fb3605-a379-56b2-ade2-3c2c9eb97061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556367} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe51a8d-5b8a-5a39-a2b7-fdd14cf223b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556371} {:answer/id #uuid "e524d323-b2cd-59f8-9307-3308a883afce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e524d323-b2cd-59f8-9307-3308a883afce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556399} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ec708fe-da2e-5933-be9e-a4bf84399625" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bcfa3740-40ac-5541-a7cc-f675d652dadd" :question/problem "![](images/27/272f91f05987c36217eeb9bcb74c4a7b9115f00dbf9358873616a09556803f4a.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{121\\sqrt{3}}{18}\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.$ Find $AC.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=\\frac{22}{3}\\text{ cm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={120}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{121\\sqrt{3}}{18} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot \\frac{22}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{121\\sqrt{3}}{18} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot \\frac{22}{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{121\\sqrt{3}}{18} & =\\frac{11\\sqrt{3}}{6}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{\\frac{121\\sqrt{3}}{18}}{\\frac{11\\sqrt{3}}{6}} \\\\ b & =\\frac{11}{3}\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=\\frac{11}{3}\\,\\text{cm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a81808-f3a7-51b7-a275-f77c8ce4630c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{37}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09a81808-f3a7-51b7-a275-f77c8ce4630c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{37}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556354} :answer-field/id #uuid "5da2ced1-509c-5f9a-a1eb-9a312a6150a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d3e9f084-c5c8-5e5d-a8cd-0b8a1cdc4b6a" :question/problem "![](images/3d/3dc502507d431d52979ff26a01089d61793a9487989d9203fd2f4f9246ecb1e3.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{111\\sqrt{2}}{32}\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.$ Then, $AC=${{field-1}} $\\text{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=\\frac{3}{2}\\text{ cm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{111\\sqrt{2}}{32} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{111\\sqrt{2}}{32} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{111\\sqrt{2}}{32} & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{8}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{\\frac{111\\sqrt{2}}{32}}{\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{8}} \\\\ b & =\\frac{37}{4}\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=\\frac{37}{4}\\,\\text{cm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556400 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "189e4e81-e91b-541e-a713-a659dc893ccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{3}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556357} {:answer/id #uuid "397621df-9258-5f5c-b429-ac0df048eae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{17}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556361} {:answer/id #uuid "56329264-d12e-5e0e-a820-46d1266fc01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556365} {:answer/id #uuid "9031717b-9f69-57af-8df6-781065968f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1445}{27}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556377} {:answer/id #uuid "9197fd42-c5ad-5145-ad0f-670e27d991b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56329264-d12e-5e0e-a820-46d1266fc01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\text{mm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556365} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0c61827-39ef-5b85-8a67-6d3bc7f90cb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e783bc5b-9008-5bdd-9a47-991be242d917" :question/problem "![](images/77/7783777fcc75d7c12a8eaed0f703bc6a864e805fc8c814860a899e3737f71480.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{85\\sqrt{2}}{36}\\,\\text{mm}^{2}.$ Find $AC.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=\\frac{17}{3}\\text{ mm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{85\\sqrt{2}}{36} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot \\frac{17}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{85\\sqrt{2}}{36} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot \\frac{17}{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{85\\sqrt{2}}{36} & =\\frac{17\\sqrt{2}}{12}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{\\frac{85\\sqrt{2}}{36}}{\\frac{17\\sqrt{2}}{12}} \\\\ b & =\\frac{5}{3}\\text{ mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\text{mm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e808f9d-31be-5a13-9482-c882618b4d13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556363} {:answer/id #uuid "6464c1a3-5522-5202-84e1-c5893469dea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556369} {:answer/id #uuid "6eeeca4d-7e83-52c4-ab5b-afef0a041e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556370} {:answer/id #uuid "8f4a94f6-3016-5f60-9d0c-0d9b1512ddce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "245\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556376} {:answer/id #uuid "bd849935-03e9-5534-b8df-54ec95c858b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6eeeca4d-7e83-52c4-ab5b-afef0a041e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556370} :answer-field/id #uuid "b93faa7e-caa0-5f1e-8af9-0757c496ba0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e802d20f-bb4d-5898-ad8c-4cb88bfe1ea8" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6a2da5468750823c21e259f6aa40a0c93342a2bebcc38a1292aa43686ca618fa.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{35}{4}\\,\\text{cm}^{2}.$ Use $\\sin30^{\\circ}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find $AC.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=7\\text{ cm} \\\\ b & =AC \\\\ m∠A & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{35}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 7\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{35}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{35}{4} & =\\frac{7}{4}b \\\\ b & =\\frac{\\frac{35}{4}}{\\frac{7}{4}} \\\\ b & =5\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=5\\,\\text{cm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a8b33db-e437-5653-925f-1fa9cfe52297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556488} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4c00d2-5098-54c4-bfb3-fb1e4e39a687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556678} {:answer/id #uuid "991cbc71-8163-5cad-9451-b64c49988e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556798} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b5b62d-cb0a-5d58-aa38-44da5f8d23e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556898} {:answer/id #uuid "d3da23ec-8498-546a-b90f-b15aaef5d70d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a4c00d2-5098-54c4-bfb3-fb1e4e39a687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556678} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b8240cb-eb83-5b67-93ee-5d39ead36d05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d2ad0a0-cc2c-5c3a-a170-bcfe4847272e" :question/math-academy-id "q-11480" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y={x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(1-x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are  $x=0$ and  $x=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=1.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x({x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={2\\pi (\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\text{ }-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{5})-0] \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{1}{20}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29951222-87d7-548a-9a5e-144857424fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556519} {:answer/id #uuid "46324b89-8e6d-58d2-acb1-a3d8251446db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556585} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff11515-d97e-5995-80f0-2ad0af75b6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556820} {:answer/id #uuid "b92f6a22-272b-56d9-abd2-b059ce0496cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556882} {:answer/id #uuid "e276d83b-7cf3-5d6b-86cd-b9f78e932f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ff11515-d97e-5995-80f0-2ad0af75b6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556820} :answer-field/id #uuid "50488e0d-3249-550c-8d98-bec9f2011aab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "228ddf28-6449-527a-a0f3-502e03cb4eba" :question/math-academy-id "q-18525" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f9738ccf12d8b9e8f3ed84870371972767e67a624f31fe5782f39679253965c4.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the graph of $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x},$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,4)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557222 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50ba80ef-f04e-50df-b6d3-a54349425c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $5$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556614} {:answer/id #uuid "99191248-3fa5-597a-8b54-a603b1f3f7cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\frac{1}{5}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556797} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e84b15-e7c8-5fd4-aa6e-1f803de91be4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A single point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556826} {:answer/id #uuid "da3da08e-ab18-5b95-a7d3-b32ef06eaf72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556972} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4ed344-c362-572c-a34e-eb57da043aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1e84b15-e7c8-5fd4-aa6e-1f803de91be4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A single point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556826} :answer-field/id #uuid "2872dfa0-d71b-51c3-93f9-e6e87fc08e6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "27762923-ddbc-55ca-b261-ca5c61feb064" :question/math-academy-id "q-95396" :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{16}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,\\underbrace{24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}}_{Q(x)}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{16}\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{64}) & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{64})-\\frac{4}{64} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8})^{2}-\\frac{1}{16} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8})^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{z}_{1}^{2}+4{z}_{2}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{5}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{4}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nA sum of squares is equal to zero if and only if each summand is zero, so the given quadratic equation represents a single point."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5126c3d8-978f-582d-85b3-1b273093c176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556615} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba1a2b2-1160-53df-9d34-b83861b916c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556764} {:answer/id #uuid "b59b2a2c-fb0b-5db0-9b18-977f28ffedf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556874} {:answer/id #uuid "d151476d-b4bb-5405-af19-fe054e71c98a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556935} {:answer/id #uuid "d70bd935-3591-5e22-b322-ce29e2d33415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d151476d-b4bb-5405-af19-fe054e71c98a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556935} :answer-field/id #uuid "64fdecf4-7be9-5050-b772-01b00e36fa6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "325cb3ef-db56-5f90-98ae-212e45702d0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109145" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c4c61c-51ad-50d2-aac0-fb3d0ca8ba37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218